# ThreadSanitizer suppressions. Wired in by the sanitizers job in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml; pass it locally with
#   TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=$PWD/scripts/tsan.supp
#
# GDAL's own lock ordering. Ubuntu's libopencv-imgcodecs links libgdal for its
# raster backends, so every phig test that decodes an image pulls it in, and
# TSan's deadlock detector reports a lock-order inversion inside GDAL's
# CPLAcquireMutex. Measured on this gate's first CI run: 20 warnings, all of
# them this one, all in libgdal.so.34, sinking 71 of 689 tests. Zero data races
# were reported -- phig's own locking came back clean on Linux/GCC, and the
# inversion is in a library phig neither owns nor calls into directly.
#
# Deliberately narrow. `called_from_lib:libgdal.so` would also silence genuine
# races reached through OpenCV; this drops only lock-order reports from that one
# function, leaving the race detector and the rest of the deadlock detector on.
deadlock:CPLAcquireMutex

# FFmpeg's internal buffer machinery, reached from compute_video_phash. Ubuntu
# ships libavcodec and libavutil uninstrumented, so TSan cannot see the atomics
# their refcounting is built on and reports every allocation they hand to the
# interceptors -- the same blindness libgomp has, documented under
# ThreadSanitizer in CLAUDE.md. Both accesses in these reports hold a mutex,
# just not one TSan can prove covers both.
#
# By library, not by symbol, because by symbol does not converge: suppressing
# av_mallocz and av_buffer_unref removed 23 reports and uncovered 23 more on
# av_frame_unref and av_frame_new_side_data_from_buf. The racy accesses are all
# allocator interceptors (free, realloc, memset) called from these libraries,
# which is what called_from_lib is for. A race in phig's own code is not called
# from them, so this does not silence one.
called_from_lib:libavcodec.so
called_from_lib:libavutil.so

# libswscale, the third FFmpeg library on that path, through OpenCV's FFmpeg
# backend, which owns the SwsContext. The two lines above make this one
# necessary. OpenCV 5 gives the SwsContext a thread per CPU, so FFmpeg runs each
# scaling pass on a slicethread. avpriv_slicethread_execute (libavutil) locks,
# waits on and unlocks done_mutex on one thread, but the unlock is compiled as a
# tail call, so its return address is libswscale's. TSan ignores the lock
# (called from libavutil), records the unlock, and reports "unlock of an
# unlocked mutex". Measured on FFmpeg 9.0.2 / OpenCV 5.0: 106 reports across the
# video keyframe tests, 0 with this line. libswscale calls no pthread_mutex_*
# itself and never calls back into phig.
called_from_lib:libswscale.so

# oneTBB, OpenCV's parallel framework on Linux, reached from any decode or
# imgproc call. TBB frees its global process affinity mask when its last
# reference goes (__TBB_InitOnce::remove_ref, misc_ex.cpp) while a thread waking
# workers memcmps it (affinity_helper::protect_affinity_mask). The reference
# count orders the two, and it is an atomic inside libtbb, which Ubuntu and Arch
# ship uninstrumented. Both accesses are interceptors (operator delete[],
# memcmp) called from libtbb on TBB's own memory, so phig code run inside a
# parallel region is still checked. OMP_NUM_THREADS does not reach TBB.
# libtbbmalloc is TBB's allocator, loaded by libtbb, and reports the same way:
# memset from two TBB workers on memory it manages.
called_from_lib:libtbb.so
called_from_lib:libtbbmalloc.so

# LibRaw::get_timestamp: strdup in one scan worker, free in another. Looked like
# a real bug at first -- raw_decode runs unserialized across workers, and
# libraw_r is meant to be the reentrant build -- so it was investigated rather
# than assumed. It is glibc, and it is safe:
#
#   1. get_timestamp calls exactly one libc function, mktime, twice. It touches
#      no global or static state of its own.
#   2. glibc's mktime calls __tzset(), which takes tzset_lock.
#   3. tzset_internal does `free (old_tz); old_tz = __strdup (tz);` -- the
#      reported strdup/free pair -- and __tzset() holds the lock across it.
#   4. tzset_lock is a __libc_lock, not a pthread mutex TSan intercepts. The
#      report shows no mutexes held, while the FFmpeg reports above show
#      "(mutexes: write M0)": TSan can see FFmpeg's locks and not glibc's.
#
# So the synchronization is there and TSan cannot see it, which is this file's
# recurring theme. POSIX requires mktime to be thread-safe, and 5 runs of
# HasherTest.DecodeWarningNotMisattributedAcrossThreads under TSan against
# LibRaw 0.22.2 reproduced nothing. phig needs no lock around RAW decode.
race:LibRaw::get_timestamp

# OpenCV DNN, reached from face detection and recognition (core/faces.cpp). Its
# parallel_for_ bodies (repackDepthwiseConvWeights, resizeNearest,
# transformLayoutInterleave_32u) run on TBB workers and memset or memcpy buffers
# the calling thread allocated; TBB's spawn and wait order the two, inside
# libtbb, where TSan cannot see it. Measured: every one of 22 reports had a TBB
# worker on one side, and forcing parallel_for_ serial (cv::setNumThreads(0))
# removed all of them. phig code never runs inside DNN, so its own accesses are
# still checked; what is lost is a DNN read racing a phig write to the same Mat.
called_from_lib:libopencv_dnn.so

# OpenCV imgcodecs' codec registry, reached from generate_thumb through
# cv::imwrite. getCodecs() is a function-local static. The first thread
# constructs it under __cxa_guard_acquire, which TSan models; every later thread
# takes the inline fast path, a plain load of the guard byte compiled into the
# uninstrumented library, so TSan records no acquire and reports the next
# thread's copy of BmpEncoder's description string. Measured: 6 of 30 runs of
# ThumbsEngineRun.GeneratesOneThumbnailPerSelectedImage. Function-name rules do
# not match: TSan resolves no symbols in a stripped libopencv.
called_from_lib:libopencv_imgcodecs.so
